WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:05.720
Democratic Senator Elizabeth Warren, who is
running for president as well, has released

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:06.720
a plan

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:11.370
or how to pay for Medicare for all. And we're
going to go through it and we're going to

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:16.391
break it down, but we do need some important
context in order to do this correctly. One

00:00:16.391 --> 00:00:22.039
controversy around Elizabeth Warren and Medicare
for all is that there is a criticism of her

00:00:22.039 --> 00:00:27.410
that she's not really for Medicare for all
at all, but that maybe she's open to it along

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:32.230
with lots of other ideas, which are actually
the ones that she would pursue. And if that's

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:38.149
true, then that is absolutely a concern. Now,
lately I'm seeing her talk more and more about

00:00:38.149 --> 00:00:43.699
how Medicare for all is the way being more
and more for it. Even in supposedly leaked

00:00:43.699 --> 00:00:47.780
behind the scenes, private video talking to
union leaders, she still was talking about

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:52.280
how Medicare for all she believes is the way
to get the most care to the most people, the

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:55.139
most cheap, uh, the, the, the in the most
affordable way possible.

00:00:55.139 --> 00:00:59.440
So that's number one. And certainly putting
out a plan for how to pay for it is a good

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:06.509
sign that she's taking it seriously. Second
piece of context, there are questions specifically

00:01:06.509 --> 00:01:10.700
around the cost of Medicare for all that I've
brought up. Medicare for all can still be

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:16.280
the best plan. I still support Medicare for
all, but at the same time, if there are unanswered

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:19.990
questions, if there are real criticisms of
it, not dumb ones like the U S has a lot of

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:24.020
people so it wouldn't work. That's a dumb
criticism. We've already talked about that

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:28.990
there are real criticisms and on the left
we want to be prepared to address and deal

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:32.409
with those. Now I know that there are people
on the left who will say, but David, nothing

00:01:32.409 --> 00:01:36.350
on the right ever has to be perfect and they
do it anyway, so why does everything we do

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:37.350
have to be perfect?

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:40.759
It doesn't have to be perfect and in fact
it's not, and it may still still be the best

00:01:40.759 --> 00:01:46.700
thing and the one the path most worth pursuing.
But we should not pretend that these questions

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:51.780
don't exist because we want to be equipped
to answer them. So to remind you the problems

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:59.049
with most of the prior plans for how to pay
for Medicare for all are mostly twofold. Problem.

00:01:59.049 --> 00:02:04.289
One is that many of the plans that say here's
how much it will cost and how we pay for it,

00:02:04.289 --> 00:02:10.640
they are assuming a drastic reduction in healthcare
costs in the U S to the levels of Australia

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.760
and Canada, which are about half of ours in
order to make the numbers work. But they don't

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:20.680
say how they would do that. They say we will
do it, but they don't say how. And the reality

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:26.790
is that the health insurance bureaucracy that
we have does add costs to the system.

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:30.580
So if you get rid of that with a Medicare
for all system, you do shaved some of the

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:35.760
costs, but only a little bit of what you would
need to shave in order to cut the price of

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:41.530
our care delivery by 50% which is what Australia
and Canada have. If you can't explain how

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:45.350
to do that, the numbers don't work. That's
a problem with a lot of the plans that are

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:51.720
out there. Number two, the other issue is
that a lot of the plans, um, for Medicare

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:58.240
for all include these phrases that nobody
in the middle class is going to pay more in

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:03.940
the net, but we've not seen the middle-class
defined and you can get pretty slippery with

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:08.990
that. And then lastly, there's also the question
of would Elizabeth Warren or anyone else's

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:15.420
plan make private insurance illegal as an
industry? Or would it simply say, let whatever

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:20.100
happens to private insurance happen if people
want it, okay, it'll probably go up in cost

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.270
as people get out of it.

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:26.370
And that's it. And I tend to favor that. I
think that it's the, I think it's legally

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:30.720
difficult to say this industry can no longer
exist anymore. So I think the, as they like

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:34.490
to say, the free market solution should be
applied. If hundreds of millions of people

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:39.640
get out of private insurance, I think the
problem will sort of solve itself. Okay, so

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:44.000
let's look at Elizabeth Warren's plan for
how to pay for Medicare for all. Now as we

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:49.120
look at this list, understand that lots of
this is controversial and I'm not going to

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:56.620
hide that from you. First of all, Warren estimates
the cost at 20 point $5 trillion in additional

00:03:56.620 --> 00:04:01.850
spending by the federal government over 10
years. This is much lower than what the urban

00:04:01.850 --> 00:04:07.140
Institute analysis says. They have a very
detailed projection, which says it'll cost

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:13.800
34 trillion additional over 10 years. That
alone, like if that's true, if the cost is

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:19.170
really 34 instead of 20 and a half, that already
makes Warren's math completely wrong.

00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:23.540
Now, there's already some confusion about
these numbers. On the one hand, she says it'll

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:28.280
cost 20 point 5 trillion. But on the other
hand, she says on our website that it's a

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:33.530
$52 trillion over 10 year plan, which is it?
It's sort of both. And this is confusing and

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:39.150
it relates to the fact that some of the existing
costs paid by consumers would instead stay

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:44.430
in the pockets of consumers. But then there
will be new spending that has to make up for

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:50.690
that, which is what they call a additional
federal spending. But the idea is either way,

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:55.110
the baseline is 52 trillion over 10 years.
In one case, the government is in the middle

00:04:55.110 --> 00:05:00.340
and that's what needs to be additionally funded.
Now if you look at the savings that Elizabeth

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:06.100
Warren lists or where she would, uh, pay for,
um, uh, these, uh, where the money would come

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:10.340
from to pay for this additional spending,
she says, eight point 8 trillion comes from

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:11.420
employers.

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:16.410
Instead of paying health insurance premiums
to private insurers, they start paying the

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:21.090
government through a payroll tax. That's eight
point 8 trillion. 2 trillion comes from taxing

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:26.022
the investment gains of the top 1%. 1 trillion
comes from a wealth tax on billionaires. 800

00:05:26.022 --> 00:05:33.270
billion comes from taxes on financial transactions,
and then six point 1 trillion is contributed

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:40.770
by the States. That adds up. Uh, and then,
uh, she also, uh, you know, is assuming drastically

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:46.700
lower costs, which we're going to talk about
in a moment. These numbers add up if you believe

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:52.880
the additional spending is 20 and a half trillion
instead of the 34 trillion, which the urban

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:58.960
Institute says it will. And that gets us to
the assumptions about lower prescription drug

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:04.600
prices and reducing administrative spending,
but more importantly, reducing what we pay

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:10.870
for healthcare period. And this is where her
plan falls short. This is not unique to Elizabeth

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:11.870
Warren.

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:17.400
She assumes that health care costs will number
one drop to be much closer to those of other

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:22.460
countries. And number two, grow much more
slowly than anticipated. You have to defend

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:29.040
those assumptions because they are huge, complicated
things. And as yet she doesn't appropriately

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:35.030
defend them in her plan. This is not me being
a Warren attacker nor a Warren shill. This

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:40.110
is me just looking at the data and the criticisms.
If you look at Elizabeth Warren's plan on

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:47.160
her website, it is assumed as a foregone conclusion
that healthcare spending drops drastically

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:52.040
and rapidly and almost immediately when you
switch to Medicare for all. I already told

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:57.310
you in my general analysis, L analysis of
how to pay for Medicare for all that serious

00:06:57.310 --> 00:07:01.671
experts, even experts on the left say that
these expectations are unrealistic. There

00:07:01.671 --> 00:07:07.310
are estimates that in the best of cases, it
could take decades to tamp down healthcare

00:07:07.310 --> 00:07:11.240
costs in the U S to make 20 point 5 trillion.

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:16.630
A realistic number, a more realistic number
is 34 trillion as cited by the urban Institute.

00:07:16.630 --> 00:07:22.500
So to be completely Frank, this is a fine
plan. It's not drastically different than

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:29.240
most of the other plans that we've seen. And
it's, it's a problem is it counts on drastic

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:35.310
reductions in healthcare spending that cannot
be assumed unless you explain why is the urban

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:40.850
Institute and other economists that have looked
at this, why are they wrong and why is Elizabeth

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:45.639
Warren, right? Or why is Bernie right about
the drastic reductions in spending? Because

00:07:45.639 --> 00:07:51.110
you can't attribute it simply to changing
systems. Simply going from a private insurance

00:07:51.110 --> 00:07:56.860
system to Medicare for all doesn't drop healthcare
spending by the 50% it would need to drop

00:07:56.860 --> 00:08:03.150
to match that of Australia or Canada. All
this being said, Medicare for all may still

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:09.230
be the best plan even at 34 trillion in additional
spending over 10 years, but we've got to figure

00:08:09.230 --> 00:08:10.360
out how to pay for that.

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:14.740
It's doable, but we just have to be honest
about the numbers and this is now the trend

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:20.110
that the pro proposals that are being put
forward for how to pay for it have extraordinarily

00:08:20.110 --> 00:08:25.180
optimistic numbers about the costs, which
serious think tanks and economists simply

00:08:25.180 --> 00:08:30.590
don't agree with that being said, Warren being
more and more behind Medicare for all. That's

00:08:30.590 --> 00:08:35.499
good, but we've got resolve this issue because
otherwise it either will not happen even with

00:08:35.499 --> 00:08:39.779
a democratic president who, or it'll happen
and we will be shocked at how much more it

00:08:39.779 --> 00:08:44.199
will cost, which is also a problem. If we're
going to do it, let's do it right based on

00:08:44.199 --> 00:08:47.121
what it'll actually cost. We've got to deal
with it at some point in time and I hope it

00:08:47.121 --> 00:08:47.129
gets dealt with soon.

